---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE, IMAGES } from '../data/site.ts';

const title = 'Shipping & Logistics from Iran — Ports, Incoterms, Transit Times | Palude';
const description =
  'Iranian export shipping and logistics: exit ports (Bandar Abbas, Bushehr, Anzali), Incoterms FOB/CIF/CFR/DAP, transit times to EU, GCC, Russia, South Asia and East Asia, required transport documents and reefer cold-chain.';

const serviceSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: title,
  url: `${SITE.url}/shipping-and-logistics/`,
  description,
  about: {
    '@type': 'Service',
    name: 'Iranian Export Shipping and Logistics',
    provider: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name, url: SITE.url },
    serviceType: 'Export freight forwarding and logistics coordination',
    areaServed: 'Worldwide',
  },
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={serviceSchema}>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Shipping & Logistics', href: '/shipping-and-logistics/' }]} />

  <section class="container-wide py-14 sm:py-20">
    <header class="max-w-3xl">
      <p class="mb-3 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-accent">Shipping &amp; logistics</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-extrabold leading-tight text-foreground text-balance sm:text-5xl">
        Iranian exit ports, Incoterms and transit times for B2B export
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-foreground/80 text-pretty">
        Logistics is the export discipline that determines whether a contracted shipment arrives at
        the destination port on the timeline the buyer planned, at the cost the buyer budgeted, and
        in the condition the buyer specified. This page documents the Iranian exit ports we ship
        from, the Incoterms we work with, the transit times to the major destination markets, and
        the transport documents every shipment carries.
      </p>
    </header>

    <figure class="mt-10 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-border shadow-sm">
      <img
        src={IMAGES.logistics}
        alt="Container port handling Iranian export cargo for international B2B shipping"
        class="h-72 w-full object-cover sm:h-80"
        loading="eager"
        width="940"
        height="650"
      />
      <figcaption class="bg-secondary/60 px-4 py-2.5 text-center text-sm text-muted-foreground">
        Bandar Abbas is the primary Iranian exit port for Gulf, South Asian and European container traffic
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[1.4fr_0.9fr] lg:items-start">
      <article class="prose-palude max-w-none">
        <h2>Iranian exit ports</h2>
        <p>
          Iran's export cargo ships from three principal port systems, each serving a different
          geography. Choosing the exit port is a destination-routing decision, and we select the
          port that produces the shortest transit and the lowest landed cost for the buyer's
          specific destination.
        </p>
        <h3>Bandar Abbas</h3>
        <p>
          Bandar Abbas, on the Strait of Hormuz, is Iran's largest container port and the default
          exit port for Gulf, South Asian, European and East Asian container traffic. The port
          handles the majority of our reefer (refrigerated) container traffic for
          <a href="/export/mazafati-dates-bulk-import/">Mazafati dates</a> and the dry-container
          traffic for <a href="/export/bulk-fandoghi-pistachios-wholesale/">pistachios</a>,
          <a href="/export/iranian-saffron-super-negin/">saffron</a> and
          <a href="/export/golden-almonds-iran-sourcing/">almonds</a>. Most major container lines
          call at Bandar Abbas, which gives us schedule flexibility for non-urgent shipments and
          the option of direct or transhipment routing depending on the destination.
        </p>
        <h3>Bushehr</h3>
        <p>
          Bushehr, on the Gulf coast west of Bandar Abbas, is the secondary Gulf port and is
          particularly useful for shipments to Iraqi, Kuwaiti and eastern Saudi destinations, where
          the shorter Gulf crossing produces a lower freight cost and a shorter transit than
          routing through Bandar Abbas. Bushehr also handles a portion of the
          <a href="/export/persian-travertine-marble-supplier/">stone and bitumen</a> export traffic,
          where the heavy-cargo handling capability is relevant.
        </p>
        <h3>Anzali</h3>
        <p>
          Anzali, on the Caspian Sea in Gilan province, is the exit port for the overland-and-Caspian
          route to Russia and the CIS. Cargo from Anzali ships by Caspian Sea vessel to Astrakhan
          (Russia) or Aktau (Kazakhstan), or by overland reefer truck through Azerbaijan to Moscow.
          The Anzali route is the fastest route to Moscow for time-sensitive
          <a href="/export/bulk-fandoghi-pistachios-wholesale/">pistachio shipments to Russia</a>,
          with a 10-14 day transit from our Urmia base to the Moscow distribution point.
        </p>

        <h2>Incoterms: FOB, CFR, CIF and DAP</h2>
        <p>
          We work with the ICC Incoterms 2020 rules, and the four terms most often used in Iranian
          export contracts are FOB, CFR, CIF and DAP. The choice of Incoterms is a risk-and-cost
          allocation decision, and we quote the term the buyer specifies or recommend the term that
          best fits the buyer's logistics capability.
        </p>
        <h3>FOB (Free On Board)</h3>
        <p>
          Under FOB, Palude delivers the cargo, cleared for export, alongside the vessel at the
          named Iranian port (typically Bandar Abbas). The buyer's nominated carrier takes the cargo
          from that point and bears the ocean freight and destination costs. FOB is the term
          preferred by buyers who have their own freight-forwarding relationships and want to
          control the ocean-leg cost and routing.
        </p>
        <h3>CFR (Cost and Freight)</h3>
        <p>
          Under CFR, Palude delivers the cargo to the named destination port, paying the ocean
          freight, but the buyer bears the cargo insurance and the destination clearance risk from
          the moment the cargo is loaded at the Iranian port. CFR is the term for buyers who want
          us to handle the ocean freight but who have their own destination broker and insurance
          arrangement.
        </p>
        <h3>CIF (Cost, Insurance and Freight)</h3>
        <p>
          Under CIF, Palude delivers the cargo to the named destination port, paying both the ocean
          freight and the marine cargo insurance. The buyer bears the destination clearance risk.
          CIF is the term most often used by buyers who want a single delivered-to-port price and
          who prefer the marine insurance to be arranged at the origin side.
        </p>
        <h3>DAP (Delivered at Place)</h3>
        <p>
          Under DAP, Palude delivers the cargo to the named destination place (typically the buyer's
          bonded warehouse), bearing all costs and risks up to that point except destination
          clearance. DAP is the term used for the overland Russia route and for destinations where
          the buyer wants a single delivered price to their warehouse door.
        </p>

        <h2>Transit times by destination</h2>
        <p>
          The transit times below are indicative, from vessel sailing at the Iranian exit port to
          arrival at the destination port. Actual transit times vary by carrier, sailing schedule
          and transhipment routing.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Bandar Abbas to Jebel Ali (UAE):</strong> 5-7 days</li>
          <li><strong>Bandar Abbas to Dammam (Saudi):</strong> 7-10 days</li>
          <li><strong>Bandar Abbas to Mundra / Nhava Sheva (India):</strong> 10-14 days</li>
          <li><strong>Bandar Abbas to Hamburg / Rotterdam (EU):</strong> 25-35 days via Suez</li>
          <li><strong>Bandar Abbas to Shanghai / Qingdao (China):</strong> 30-40 days</li>
          <li><strong>Anzali to Astrakhan (Russia, Caspian Sea):</strong> 5-8 days</li>
          <li><strong>Urmia to Moscow (overland reefer truck via Azerbaijan):</strong> 10-14 days</li>
          <li><strong>Air freight, Tehran to Europe:</strong> 3-6 days (for urgent sample and high-value shipments)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          For refrigerated cargo — Mazafati dates and any temperature-sensitive consolidation — the
          reefer container's set-point and the data-logger record are part of the shipment
          specification. Our <a href="/blog/shipping-dates-from-iran-cold-chain/">cold-chain
          logistics guide</a> covers the reefer set-points and consolidation rules in detail.
        </p>

        <h2>Required transport documents</h2>
        <p>
          Every shipment, regardless of Incoterms, travels with a transport document that evidences
          receipt of the cargo and the contract of carriage. For sea freight, the document is the
          bill of lading issued by the carrier. For air freight, it is the airway bill. For overland
          shipment to Russia and the CIS, it is the CMR consignment note. The transport document is
          also the document of title for letter-of-credit transactions — the buyer's bank pays
          against the presented transport document and the accompanying commercial documents.
        </p>
        <p>
          The full document set — commercial invoice, packing list, certificate of origin,
          product-specific certificates, transport document, and (for hazardous cargo) the MSDS and
          hazardous-cargo declaration — is covered in our
          <a href="/blog/iran-export-documentation-checklist/">Iran export documentation
          checklist</a>. The document set is checked at origin before the vessel sails, so any
          discrepancy is corrected at the loading port rather than at the destination — a step that
          prevents the destination-port demurrage that accrues when documents are corrected by email
          after the cargo has sailed.
        </p>

        <h2>Insurance and the temperature-logger record</h2>
        <p>
          Marine cargo insurance is arranged either under CIF terms (by Palude, at the origin side)
          or under FOB/CFR/DAP terms (by the buyer, at the destination side). For reefer cargo, we
          arrange transit insurance through a London-market specialist marine cargo policy that
          covers temperature-excursion damage — the loss that occurs when a reefer loses set-point
          in transit. The reefer's data logger is the evidence of the temperature history and the
          document that supports a temperature-excursion claim. A buyer who ships reefer cargo under
          a general cargo policy is a buyer whose temperature-excursion loss is not covered.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the payment-channel framework that determines when and how the transport documents are
          released, our <a href="/blog/payment-methods-iranian-exports-b2b/">payment methods for
          Iranian exports</a> guide covers the working channels. To begin a shipment with the
          routing, Incoterms and document set defined upfront, <a href="/request-a-quote/">request a
          sourcing quote</a> with your product, volume, destination port and preferred Incoterms.
        </p>
      </article>

      <aside class="space-y-5 lg:sticky lg:top-24">
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-border bg-secondary/50 p-6">
          <h2 class="font-display text-base font-bold text-foreground">Exit ports at a glance</h2>
          <dl class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm">
            <div><dt class="font-semibold text-foreground">Bandar Abbas</dt><dd class="text-muted-foreground">Gulf, South Asia, EU, East Asia container traffic</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="font-semibold text-foreground">Bushehr</dt><dd class="text-muted-foreground">Iraq, Kuwait, eastern Saudi; heavy-cargo stone and bitumen</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="font-semibold text-foreground">Anzali</dt><dd class="text-muted-foreground">Caspian Sea to Russia and CIS; overland to Moscow</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-border bg-background p-6 shadow-sm">
          <h2 class="font-display text-base font-bold text-foreground">Plan a shipment</h2>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-foreground/70">Share your product, volume, destination port and Incoterms. We respond with routing, transit time and a draft proforma.</p>
          <a href="/request-a-quote/" class="mt-4 inline-flex rounded-lg bg-primary px-5 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-primary-foreground shadow-sm transition-all hover:bg-primary/90 hover:shadow">
            Request a quote
          </a>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
